esents one whole. Insert the missing numbers in the division problem below.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$÷\\,\\frac{1}{4}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $2$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\frac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $8$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $2$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $8$ pieces.\n\nHence, the division problem is\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{1}{4}=8.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59dc96d2-9a4e-503c-9723-af6218254625" "answer-field-343762ec-f000-5e6e-ad75-37a613fc2817"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59dc96d2-9a4e-503c-9723-af6218254625" :answer-field/id #uuid "59dc96d2-9a4e-503c-9723-af6218254625" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-370ac109-9603-5801-8f92-42b44749e7f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-370ac109-9603-5801-8f92-42b44749e7f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-370ac109-9603-5801-8f92-42b44749e7f3" :answer/id #uuid "370ac109-9603-5801-8f92-42b44749e7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-343762ec-f000-5e6e-ad75-37a613fc2817" :answer-field/id #uuid "343762ec-f000-5e6e-ad75-37a613fc2817" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07" :answer/id #uuid "4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-12733436-3403-5936-b77d-c6356ea8e2b3" :question/id #uuid "12733436-3403-5936-b77d-c6356ea8e2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-59841" :question/problem "![](images/64/6463c24cede2d72af4574e9bc1ed15d09d6257e0a4e3393e52f74eddf874f862.png)\n\nEach of the two shapes in the model above represents one whole. From left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,\\,}÷\\frac{1}{2}=\\boxed{\\,\\,}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $2$ shapes. Each shape consists of $2$ pieces that each represent $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a whole. There are $4$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $2$ wholes into halves, we will be left with $4$ pieces.\n\nHence, the division problem is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,2\\,}÷\\frac{1}{2}=\\boxed{\\,4\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $2$ and $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-875bb280-5fd5-5ed7-9546-d2ffab9d4f2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-875bb280-5fd5-5ed7-9546-d2ffab9d4f2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "875bb280-5fd5-5ed7-9546-d2ffab9d4f2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4e6c36e-1810-5c18-a8a8-8dc32b1cd6a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6573b2ed-bdd5-50d2-a3a3-b779cc223e8f" "answer-2030409d-4de7-5b60-82e1-ea070a92b04d" "answer-e4e6c36e-1810-5c18-a8a8-8dc32b1cd6a9" "answer-b18a24df-3c48-5f74-b5b4-2e60241bb1bc" "answer-f2dabce0-e99d-523f-b0b0-aa291d42dcec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6573b2ed-bdd5-50d2-a3a3-b779cc223e8f" :answer/id #uuid "6573b2ed-bdd5-50d2-a3a3-b779cc223e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2030409d-4de7-5b60-82e1-ea070a92b04d" :answer/id #uuid "2030409d-4de7-5b60-82e1-ea070a92b04d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4e6c36e-1810-5c18-a8a8-8dc32b1cd6a9" :answer/id #uuid "e4e6c36e-1810-5c18-a8a8-8dc32b1cd6a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b18a24df-3c48-5f74-b5b4-2e60241bb1bc" :answer/id #uuid "b18a24df-3c48-5f74-b5b4-2e60241bb1bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2dabce0-e99d-523f-b0b0-aa291d42dcec" :answer/id #uuid "f2dabce0-e99d-523f-b0b0-aa291d42dcec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a222c00-c53f-5553-bfce-020d55c30819" :question/id #uuid "1a222c00-c53f-5553-bfce-020d55c30819" :question/math-academy-id "q-59863" :question/problem "![](images/92/9298eb33dbbe9cfc93712c86370ba589667f9512df85bece397733f85b48a8d7.png)\n\nThe shape in the model above represents $1$ whole. Using this model, find the value of $1÷\\frac{1}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate $1÷\\frac{1}{8}$, we need to find how many times $\\frac{1}{8}$ fits into a whole. \n\n![](images/5d/5d97a785920930122759fa9eae688ac30244d54728720aff6f5c7dab21d05fc4.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can see that we get $1\\times 8=8$ parts in total. Therefore:\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{1}{8}=8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfc42dec-da57-53b1-9515-aa089deadd51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfc42dec-da57-53b1-9515-aa089deadd51" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfc42dec-da57-53b1-9515-aa089deadd51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef203481-5103-53f6-bbfa-2a62808bf682" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8f3ad67-64ee-515e-8f56-2665acd0fa5a" "answer-e5bbdfc2-9a46-561f-825c-303724a3208c" "answer-cd3b6376-8e4f-5c46-bf13-517082472202" "answer-ef203481-5103-53f6-bbfa-2a62808bf682" "answer-a81f729d-2453-5e72-965f-0fe0c5d6bc0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8f3ad67-64ee-515e-8f56-2665acd0fa5a" :answer/id #uuid "a8f3ad67-64ee-515e-8f56-2665acd0fa5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5bbdfc2-9a46-561f-825c-303724a3208c" :answer/id #uuid "e5bbdfc2-9a46-561f-825c-303724a3208c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3b6376-8e4f-5c46-bf13-517082472202" :answer/id #uuid "cd3b6376-8e4f-5c46-bf13-517082472202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef203481-5103-53f6-bbfa-2a62808bf682" :answer/id #uuid "ef203481-5103-53f6-bbfa-2a62808bf682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a81f729d-2453-5e72-965f-0fe0c5d6bc0a" :answer/id #uuid "a81f729d-2453-5e72-965f-0fe0c5d6bc0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3cee5cd-3b4b-5a2f-9ea4-2689dbbea9cb" :question/id #uuid "f3cee5cd-3b4b-5a2f-9ea4-2689dbbea9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-59609" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b2aab319e386e3ffa85720f9f32b5acb03f6c8f2518c97676ba617bc2d70888.png)\n\nThe model above represents $2$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $2÷\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate $2÷\\frac{1}{4}$, we need to divide each of $2$ wholes into $4$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9165eae776ebb82d9f9f577cd44b1f780e3ba44d8ff64eed9940ef9a9f943815.png)\n\n \n\nWe get $2\\times 4=8$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{1}{4}=8\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ccf91362-3864-555d-81aa-6e0718329cfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccf91362-3864-555d-81aa-6e0718329cfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf91362-3864-555d-81aa-6e0718329cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9622d12-7295-543b-bc1e-212dca131905" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8430de0e-935d-55e0-b040-0d3e5fe1615b" "answer-6356133e-89ba-59a8-9a18-18443c69668f" "answer-e9622d12-7295-543b-bc1e-212dca131905" "answer-8f04c205-f5b8-533b-a8e9-61d26c5bc4f4" "answer-fe830b0a-25a6-5664-a804-5d14ffc40fc7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8430de0e-935d-55e0-b040-0d3e5fe1615b" :answer/id #uuid "8430de0e-935d-55e0-b040-0d3e5fe1615b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6356133e-89ba-59a8-9a18-18443c69668f" :answer/id #uuid "6356133e-89ba-59a8-9a18-18443c69668f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9622d12-7295-543b-bc1e-212dca131905" :answer/id #uuid "e9622d12-7295-543b-bc1e-212dca131905" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f04c205-f5b8-533b-a8e9-61d26c5bc4f4" :answer/id #uuid "8f04c205-f5b8-533b-a8e9-61d26c5bc4f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe830b0a-25a6-5664-a804-5d14ffc40fc7" :answer/id #uuid "fe830b0a-25a6-5664-a804-5d14ffc40fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075043 :tutorial/content "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator. \n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions. \n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\n \n\nSince each part is $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$ of a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get ${\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5$ parts in total." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075044 :tutorial/content "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073949 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
